    <!-- Main Article Body -->
    <article id="article-content" class="flex-1 min-w-0 w-full space-y-16 transition-all duration-300">

      <!-- Section 1 -->
      <section id="problema" class="space-y-6 pt-2">
        <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-3">
          <span class="text-xs font-mono font-bold text-emerald-700 dark:text-emerald-400 uppercase tracking-wider">Section 1</span>
          <h2 class="text-2xl sm:text-3xl font-extrabold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 mt-1">The Problem: The Farmacy Food Case</h2>
        </div>

        <p class="leading-relaxed text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 text-base">
          In the fall of 2020, O'Reilly hosted its inaugural Architecture Kata centered on an actual commercial enterprise: Farmacy Food. Based in Detroit, the startup prepares fresh, healthy meals in commercial ghost kitchens (closed-door prep facilities without customer-facing dining rooms) and distributes ready-to-eat dishes through two distinct physical retail channels with contrasting technical requirements:
        </p>

        <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2.5 text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 text-sm sm:text-base">
          <li>
            <span class="font-semibold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100">Smart Fridges (autonomous retail units):</span> Unattended IoT refrigerators supplied by a commercial vendor (<a href="https://www.bytetechnology.com/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="text-emerald-700 dark:text-emerald-400 underline font-medium">Byte Technology</a>). Customers swipe a payment card to release the magnetic door latch, take whatever meals they choose (weight sensors and RFID tags detect items removed in real time), and upon closing the door, the system settles the payment automatically without cashiers.
          </li>
          <li>
            <span class="font-semibold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100">Assisted Kiosks:</span> Standard refrigerators placed within partnered coffee shops, gyms, or co-working spaces, equipped with third-party POS counter terminals (<a href="https://pos.toasttab.com/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="text-emerald-700 dark:text-emerald-400 underline font-medium">Toast POS</a>). Customers pick their food, and an on-site store employee processes the sale at the counter register.
          </li>
        </ul>

        <Mermaid code={contextDiagram} title="System Context Diagram for Farmacy Food" />

        <div class="bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 rounded-xl p-5 border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 space-y-2.5 shadow-xs transition-colors">
          <div class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 font-mono uppercase tracking-wider text-xs">Core Business Constraints</div>
          <p>• <span class="font-semibold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100">Day 1 Scale:</span> Only 2 pilot locations in Detroit handling ~300 meals per week. Near-zero initial traffic.</p>
          <p>• <span class="font-semibold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100">12-Month Target:</span> Expand to 8 locations during the first half of the year, finishing year one with 68 retail locations, 2,000 weekly meals, and 1,000 active subscribers.</p>
          <p>• <span class="font-semibold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100">Out of Scope:</span> Physical delivery van routing logistics and internal fridge firmware were explicitly excluded from the platform architecture.</p>
          <p>• <span class="font-semibold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100">Integration Challenges:</span> How to validate customer profiles to apply meal subsidies in a fridge lacking screens or keypads? How to ensure offline sales during kiosk network outages using <ConceptChip id="store-and-forward" label="Offline Mode & Store-and-Forward" icon="📡" />? How to prevent two customers from claiming the last meal simultaneously without deadlocks using <ConceptChip id="optimistic-concurrency" label="Optimistic Concurrency Control" icon="🛡️" />?</p>
        </div>

        <p class="leading-relaxed text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 text-sm sm:text-base mt-4">
          Faced with these specifications, participating teams confronted a classic dilemma: design an austere, lightweight system to validate product viability with two initial fridges, or construct an elastic distributed platform capable of handling the projected 68 locations from Day 1? Each team drew its own architectural boundaries, reflected in the overall competition standings.
        </p>
      </section>

      <!-- Section 2 -->
      <section id="tablero" class="space-y-6 pt-2">
        <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-3">
          <span class="text-xs font-mono font-bold text-emerald-700 dark:text-emerald-400 uppercase tracking-wider">Section 2</span>
          <h2 class="text-2xl sm:text-3xl font-extrabold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 mt-1">The Global Podium: Cross-Team Comparative Matrix</h2>
        </div>

        <p class="leading-relaxed text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 text-sm sm:text-base">
          Before exploring specific technical decisions, the following matrix compares the core approaches and principal trade-offs documented by the teams in their open repositories on <a href="https://github.com/TheKataLog" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="text-emerald-700 dark:text-emerald-400 underline font-mono font-medium">TheKataLog</a>:
        </p>

        <div class="overflow-x-auto rounded-xl border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 shadow-xs transition-colors">
          <table class="w-full text-left text-xs border-collapse">
            <thead>
              <tr class="bg-slate-50 dark:bg-slate-950 text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 font-mono">
                <th class="p-3 w-[14%] whitespace-nowrap">Team</th>
                <th class="p-3 w-[14%] whitespace-nowrap">Standing</th>
                <th class="p-3 w-[18%]">Foundational Style</th>
                <th class="p-3 w-[16%]">Domain Strategy</th>
                <th class="p-3 w-[16%]">Communication</th>
                <th class="p-3 w-[22%]">Primary Trade-off Assumed</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody class="divide-y divide-slate-200/80 dark:divide-slate-800 font-sans text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300">
              <tr class="bg-emerald-50/40 dark:bg-emerald-950/20">
                <td class="p-3 font-bold text-emerald-900 dark:text-emerald-300 font-mono whitespace-nowrap">
                  <a href="https://github.com/TheKataLog/ArchColider" target="_blank" class="hover:underline">ArchColider</a>
                </td>
                <td class="p-3 whitespace-nowrap"><span class="inline-block whitespace-nowrap px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full bg-emerald-100 dark:bg-emerald-950/70 text-emerald-800 dark:text-emerald-300 font-semibold font-mono border border-emerald-300 dark:border-emerald-700">🥇 1st Place</span></td>
                <td class="p-3 font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100">Modular Monolith (AWS EC2 / ASG)</td>
                <td class="p-3">DDD: Core, Supporting, Generic</td>
                <td class="p-3">Async (RabbitMQ) + Public Facades</td>
                <td class="p-3 text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400">Risk of architectural decay into a Big Ball of Mud without automated boundary checks.</td>
              </tr>
              <tr class="bg-slate-50/50 dark:bg-slate-900/50">
                <td class="p-3 font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 font-mono whitespace-nowrap">
                  <a href="https://github.com/TheKataLog/Myagis-Forest" target="_blank" class="hover:underline">Myagis-Forest</a>
                </td>
                <td class="p-3 whitespace-nowrap"><span class="inline-block whitespace-nowrap px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full bg-slate-200 dark:bg-slate-800 text-slate-800 dark:text-slate-300 font-semibold font-mono border border-slate-300 dark:border-slate-700">🥈 2nd Place</span></td>
                <td class="p-3 font-medium text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-200">Microservices on K8s (EKS)</td>
                <td class="p-3">Context Map DDD (Inventory / Orders)</td>
                <td class="p-3">
                  <span>Domain Events + BFF Pattern</span>
                  <span class="block text-[11px] text-slate-500 dark:text-slate-400 font-sans font-normal">Dedicated adapters per consumer</span>
                </td>
                <td class="p-3 text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400">Eventual consistency under CQRS; heavy operational overhead of Kubernetes for two initial fridges.</td>
              </tr>
              <tr class="bg-slate-50/30 dark:bg-slate-900/30">
                <td class="p-3 font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 font-mono whitespace-nowrap">
                  <a href="https://github.com/TheKataLog/Jedis" target="_blank" class="hover:underline">Jedis</a>
                </td>
                <td class="p-3 whitespace-nowrap"><span class="inline-block whitespace-nowrap px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full bg-stone-200/80 dark:bg-stone-800 text-stone-800 dark:text-stone-300 font-semibold font-mono border border-stone-300 dark:border-stone-700">🥉 3rd Place</span></td>
