        <p class="leading-relaxed text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 text-sm sm:text-base">
          <span class="font-semibold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100">Economic and operational rationale:</span> For an initial scale of 68 locations and 1,000 subscribers, managing a distributed cluster introduces disproportionate infrastructure expenses and observability overhead. By adopting a modular monolith, ArchColider structured the platform as a single <em>architectural quantum</em>: all functional modules share the same deployment lifecycle and relational persistence on managed instances (AWS EC2 inside Auto-Scaling Groups). This choice minimizes initial operational complexity and neutralizes distributed network failure risks. Should an individual module (such as the meal catalog) face independent traffic surges in the future, its clear facade boundaries permit extracting that quantum into a standalone microservice without rewriting application logic.
        </p>

        <Mermaid code={styleDecisionTree} title="Architectural Style Decision Tree" />

        <PodiumCounterpoint 
          rank={2} 
          team="Myagis-Forest" 
          title="Myagis-Forest: The Danger of Delaying Distributed Decomposition"
          githubUrl="https://github.com/TheKataLog/Myagis-Forest/blob/main/ADRs/ADR001-microservice-style.md#L30-L44"
          githubLineRange="L30-L44"
          lang="en"
        >
          <p>
            In their ADR 001, Myagis-Forest argued the opposing viewpoint, asserting that building a monolith with the intention of later decomposing it doubles mid-term architectural effort:
          </p>
          <blockquote class="border-l-2 border-slate-400 dark:border-slate-600 pl-3 my-2 text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 text-xs sm:text-sm italic">
            "In the Farmacy Food scenario, which is 'green field' to the most extent, we don't see the need to invest time in designing and implementing stage architectures (e.g., modular monolith ==&gt; service-based or macroservices ==&gt; microservice). We have good reasons to believe using the microservice style from the getgo is perfectly feasible time-wise, as well as technically and financially."
          </blockquote>
          <p>
            To manage Kubernetes complexity, they structured each service using <ConceptChip id="hexagonal-architecture" label="Hexagonal Architecture (Ports & Adapters)" icon="⬡" /> and implemented the <ConceptChip id="bff-pattern" label="Backend for Frontend (BFF) Pattern" icon="📱" />: thin translation layers formatting payloads specifically for web and kiosk clients, shielding them from internal microservice APIs.
          </p>
        </PodiumCounterpoint>

        <PodiumCounterpoint 
          rank={3} 
          team="Jedis" 
          title="Jedis: Investing in Event Streaming from Phase 1"
          githubUrl="https://github.com/TheKataLog/Jedis/blob/main/Strategy.md#L16-L19"
          githubLineRange="L16-L19"
          lang="en"
        >
          <p>
            Jedis justified their infrastructure on the premise of preparing early for large-scale data ingestion: they accepted the operational complexity of deploying Apache Kafka from Phase 1 to capture every inventory and purchase event in real time, unlocking future recommendation models and analytics.
          </p>
        </PodiumCounterpoint>

        <RunnerUpInsight 
          team="Team-Pacman" 
          tag="Cost Economics" 
          title="100% Serverless: Slashing Idle Infrastructure Costs"
          githubUrl="https://github.com/TheKataLog/Team-Pacman/blob/main/adrs/serverless-approach.md#L13-L22"
          githubSnippet="L13-L22"
          lang="en"
        >
          Team-Pacman eliminated long-running server instances: they demonstrated that an architecture leveraging AWS Lambda and DynamoDB cut infrastructure costs by 70% to 90% relative to 24/7 provisioned servers, accepting function cold starts as a calculated trade-off for zero idle spend.
        </RunnerUpInsight>

        <RunnerUpInsight 
          team="Hey-Dragon" 
          tag="Evolutionary Architecture" 
          title="3-Stage Evolutionary Architecture and Transition Risks"
          githubUrl="https://github.com/TheKataLog/Hey-Dragon/blob/main/Stage%201%20-%20Layered%20Monolith/ADR%20-%20Layered%20Monolith%20as%20a%20short%20term%20Architecture.md#L1-L20"
          githubSnippet="L1-L20"
          lang="en"
        >
          Hey-Dragon planned an evolutionary transition across three phases: Stage 1 (Layered monolith for rapid market entry) to Stage 2 (Services sharing a database) to Stage 3 (Decoupled microservices with gRPC and events). They noted the operational risk: if budget tightening occurs post-launch, systems often remain in Stage 1 indefinitely.
        </RunnerUpInsight>

        <p class="leading-relaxed text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 text-sm sm:text-base mt-4">
          Whether selecting a modular monolith or committing to microservices from the start, a platform succeeds or fails based on how its boundaries are drawn. Flawed boundaries in a monolith produce unmanageable coupling; in microservices, they create a distributed monolith. Consequently, the next decision centered on domain decomposition.
        </p>
