      <!-- Section 6 -->
      <section id="dilema-4" class="space-y-6 pt-2">
        <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-3">
          <span class="text-xs font-mono font-bold text-emerald-700 dark:text-emerald-400 uppercase tracking-wider">Section 6</span>
          <h2 class="text-2xl sm:text-3xl font-extrabold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 mt-1">Data and Consistency: Event Sourcing, CQRS, or Classical Relational Tables?</h2>
        </div>

        <TheoryBox concept="CAP/PACELC and the Hidden Overhead of Eventual Consistency" chapter="Chapter 14">
          <p>
            Eric Brewer's CAP theorem demonstrated that in the presence of a network partition, a system must choose between consistency (C) and availability (A). Daniel Abadi later formulated the <ConceptChip id="pacelc-theorem" label="PACELC Theorem" icon="⚖️" /> to explain normal, non-partitioned operational states:
          </p>
          <p class="mt-2">
            The rule dictates: if there is a partition (<em>P</em>), trade off availability (<em>A</em>) or consistency (<em>C</em>); <em>E</em>lse, under normal operation, trade off latency (<em>L</em>) or consistency (<em>C</em>). Guaranteeing strictly consistent reads across distributed nodes requires waiting for replica confirmations, increasing response times. Returning sub-millisecond responses on mobile devices often demands accepting eventual consistency.
          </p>
          <p class="mt-2">
            Additionally, event choreography grants microservices autonomy but scatters business workflows across services, complicating end-to-end debugging. Centralized orchestration makes workflows explicit at the risk of creating single points of contention.
          </p>
        </TheoryBox>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 flex items-center gap-2">
          <span>🥇 ArchColider: Auditability and Immutability via Event Sourcing</span>
        </h3>

        <p class="leading-relaxed text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 text-sm sm:text-base">
          In unattended retail environments, transaction disputes demand total auditability: if an RFID sensor misreads or a customer disputes an automated charge, mutating records with <code>UPDATE orders SET status = 'cancelled'</code> destroys critical diagnostic history. In <a href="https://github.com/TheKataLog/ArchColider/blob/master/4.ADRs/007%20Event%20sourcing%20usage.md#L1-L35" target="_blank" class="text-emerald-700 dark:text-emerald-400 underline font-mono font-semibold">ADR 007: Event sourcing usage</a>, ArchColider selected <ConceptChip id="event-sourcing" label="Event Sourcing" icon="📜" /> with EventStore. As noted in their ADR, because an append-only log is the single source of truth, relational read views need no complex backups since they can be reconstructed by replaying historical events:
        </p>

        <blockquote class="border-l-2 border-emerald-500 pl-3 my-2 text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 text-xs sm:text-sm italic">
          "Keeping only the final state of a domain entity won't help to understand how we end up with a specific state... The event sourcing approach gives us a full history of changes and can help investigate issues and users' complaints."
        </blockquote>

        <p class="leading-relaxed text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 text-sm sm:text-base">
          To decouple auxiliary tasks without threatening core checkout performance, they integrated RabbitMQ (<a href="https://github.com/TheKataLog/ArchColider/blob/master/4.ADRs/008%20At%20least%20once%20delivery%20for%20ready%20to%20pay%20order.md" target="_blank" class="text-emerald-700 dark:text-emerald-400 underline font-mono font-semibold">ADR 008</a>) with <ConceptChip id="event-delivery-guarantees" label="At-Least-Once Delivery & Idempotency" icon="📬" /> (ACKs) and automated retries.
        </p>

        <Mermaid code={posSequence} title="POS Purchase Flow, Inventory Reservation, and Asynchronous Event Dispatch" heightClass="h-[500px] sm:h-[580px]" />

        <PodiumCounterpoint 
          rank={2} 
          team="Myagis-Forest" 
          title="CQRS in Myagis-Forest: Segregating Menu Browsing from Stock Deductions"
          githubUrl="https://github.com/TheKataLog/Myagis-Forest/blob/main/ADRs/ADR005-cqrs-pattern.md"
          lang="en"
        >
          <p>
            Conversely, Myagis-Forest favored read latency by implementing <ConceptChip id="cqrs" label="CQRS" icon="🔀" /> (Command Query Responsibility Segregation): a transactional relational database processed inventory updates, while denormalized read replicas absorbed high-volume lunch-hour menu traffic, accepting slight eventual consistency in available counts to protect response times.
          </p>
        </PodiumCounterpoint>

        <RunnerUpInsight 
          team="Self-Driven Team" 
          tag="RPC vs. Events" 
          title="Synchronous REST for Instant Checkout Authorization"
          githubUrl="https://github.com/TheKataLog/Self-Driven-Team/blob/main/doc/arc/adrs/adr_013.md#L1-L25"
          githubSnippet="ADR 013"
          lang="en"
        >
          Self-Driven Team deliberately rejected asynchronous messaging for payment authorizations: they implemented direct synchronous REST calls between the kiosk register and the payment service to guarantee immediate visual confirmation for customers at the counter, reserving asynchronous queues for non-blocking post-sale notifications.
        </RunnerUpInsight>

        <p class="leading-relaxed text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 text-sm sm:text-base mt-4">
          Designing events and database schemas in the abstract is clean until the software encounters the physical world: magnetic latches, card swipes, and smart fridges with unyielding operational constraints.
        </p>
      </section>

      <!-- Section 7 -->
      <section id="dilema-5" class="space-y-6 pt-2">
        <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-3">
          <span class="text-xs font-mono font-bold text-emerald-700 dark:text-emerald-400 uppercase tracking-wider">Section 7</span>
          <h2 class="text-2xl sm:text-3xl font-extrabold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 mt-1">Physical Hardware Reality and Sensor Constraints</h2>
        </div>

        <p class="leading-relaxed text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 text-sm sm:text-base">
          The kata specifications called for dynamic pricing rules and targeted meal subsidies in Smart Fridges for qualified customer demographics. While several submissions modeled pricing engines in software, team Hananoyama began by examining the physical specifications and API docs of the fridge hardware vendor (<a href="https://github.com/TheKataLog/Hananoyama/blob/main/README.md#L100-L125" target="_blank" class="text-emerald-700 dark:text-emerald-400 underline font-mono font-semibold">Byte Technology</a>):
        </p>

        <RunnerUpInsight 
          team="Hananoyama" 
          tag="Hardware Reality Check" 
          title="The Physical Hardware Constraint Identified by Hananoyama"
          githubUrl="https://github.com/TheKataLog/Hananoyama/blob/main/README.md#L100-L125"
          githubSnippet="L100-L125"
          lang="en"
        >
          Hananoyama uncovered a decisive hardware constraint: swiping a card simultaneously unlatches the door and pre-authorizes the transaction. The smart fridge contains no touchscreens, numerical keypads, or biometric readers. Consequently, autonomous dynamic subsidies at the physical fridge are impossible; discounts must be claimed in advance via the web application before visiting the fridge.
        </RunnerUpInsight>

        <Mermaid code={fridgeParadox} title="The Physical Gesture Paradox in Smart Fridge Transactions" heightClass="h-[360px] sm:h-[420px]" />

        <RunnerUpInsight 
          team="Self-Driven Team" 
          tag="Frontend Trade-off" 
          title="Responsive Web App vs. Native Mobile App: Geolocation Trade-offs"
          githubUrl="https://github.com/TheKataLog/Self-Driven-Team/blob/main/doc/arc/adrs/adr_012.md#L1-L35"
          githubSnippet="ADR 012"
          lang="en"
        >
          In their ADR 012, Self-Driven Team selected a responsive web application over native mobile apps to minimize initial development overhead, accepting that without background mobile geolocation, nearby store discovery requires manual zip code entry.
        </RunnerUpInsight>

        <p class="leading-relaxed text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 text-sm sm:text-base mt-4">
          This collision with hardware realities proves why software architecture cannot be separated from physical constraints. Every decision brings trade-offs that must be explicitly captured and mitigated.
        </p>
      </section>

      <!-- Section 8 -->
      <section id="dilema-6" class="space-y-6 pt-2">
        <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-3">
          <span class="text-xs font-mono font-bold text-emerald-700 dark:text-emerald-400 uppercase tracking-wider">Section 8</span>
          <h2 class="text-2xl sm:text-3xl font-extrabold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 mt-1">Risk Management and Architectural Commitments in ADRs</h2>
        </div>

        <TheoryBox concept="Michael Nygard's ADR Format and Risk Storming" chapter="Chapters 18 and 19">
          <p>
            An Architectural Decision Record (ADR) captures the context, chosen approach, and consequences (trade-offs) of a significant architectural choice, establishing transparent rationale before writing code.
          </p>
          <p class="mt-2">
            <span class="font-semibold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100">Risk Storming</span> (developed by Simon Brown) is an exercise where engineering teams analyze architecture diagrams to uncover risk areas (network drops, database bottlenecks, run-away cloud bills), plotting them on a Likelihood vs. Impact grid. Unavoidable high-impact threats are documented as ADRs with explicit mitigations.
          </p>
        </TheoryBox>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 flex items-center gap-2">
          <span>🥇 ArchColider's Risk Management: Resilient System Design</span>
        </h3>

        <p class="leading-relaxed text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 text-sm sm:text-base">
          In their <a href="https://github.com/TheKataLog/ArchColider/blob/master/2.SolutionBackground/RisksAndSensitivePoints.md" target="_blank" class="text-emerald-700 dark:text-emerald-400 underline font-mono font-semibold">Risks and Sensitive Points</a> document, ArchColider pinpointed key operational vulnerabilities and paired each with an ADR mitigation:
        </p>

        <div class="space-y-3 text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300">
          <div class="p-4 rounded-xl border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 shadow-xs transition-colors">
            <span class="font-bold text-red-600 dark:text-red-400 font-mono">Risk 1: Accidental Coupling Between Internal Modules</span>
            <p class="mt-1 leading-relaxed text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400">A classic risk in monolithic systems occurs when developers bypass public domain facades to access foreign tables directly. <span class="font-semibold text-emerald-700 dark:text-emerald-400">Mitigation:</span> Automated architectural <ConceptChip id="fitness-functions" label="Fitness Functions with ArchUnit" icon="🧪" /> in the CI pipeline that fail builds if unauthorized package dependencies are detected.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="p-4 rounded-xl border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 shadow-xs transition-colors">
            <span class="font-bold text-red-600 dark:text-red-400 font-mono">Risk 2: Uncontrolled Cloud Auto-Scaling Costs</span>
            <p class="mt-1 leading-relaxed text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400">Uncapped autoscaling policies can provision excess instances during abnormal traffic bursts or error loops. <span class="font-semibold text-emerald-700 dark:text-emerald-400">Mitigation:</span> Hard ceilings on EC2 instances requiring manual intervention to approve budget expansions.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="p-4 rounded-xl border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 shadow-xs transition-colors">
            <span class="font-bold text-red-600 dark:text-red-400 font-mono">Risk 3: Network Dropouts at Physical Locations</span>
            <p class="mt-1 leading-relaxed text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400">Intermittent internet outages at retail counters would block scheduled meal pickups. <span class="font-semibold text-emerald-700 dark:text-emerald-400">Mitigation:</span> Offline pickup verification using <ConceptChip id="store-and-forward" label="Store-and-Forward & PIN Codes" icon="📡" /> (<a href="https://github.com/TheKataLog/ArchColider/blob/master/4.ADRs/011%20Every%20meal%20delivery%20has%20pick%20up%20pin%20code.md" class="text-emerald-700 dark:text-emerald-400 underline font-mono font-semibold">ADR 011</a>), validating claims locally and reconciling inventory upon reconnection.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <p class="leading-relaxed text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 text-sm sm:text-base mt-4">
          Having analyzed the technical considerations across all teams, we examine the complete winning solution: ArchColider's full blueprint and 16 ADRs.
        </p>
      </section>

