        <!-- 9.3: Concurrency and Persistence -->
        <div class="space-y-4">
          <h3 class="text-lg sm:text-xl font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 flex items-center gap-2">
            <span>9.3 Concurrency Architecture: In-Memory Fridge Actors</span>
          </h3>
          <p class="leading-relaxed text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 text-sm sm:text-base">
            To prevent two users from claiming the same meal without incurring distributed lock contention, ArchColider introduced three architectural mechanisms in their <a href="https://github.com/TheKataLog/ArchColider/blob/master/3.ViewsAndPerspectives/Concurrency.md" target="_blank" class="text-emerald-700 dark:text-emerald-400 underline font-mono font-semibold">Concurrency View</a>:
          </p>

          <div class="space-y-3 text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300">
            <div class="p-4 rounded-xl border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 shadow-xs transition-colors">
              <span class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 font-mono text-emerald-800 dark:text-emerald-400">1. In-Memory Actor per Physical Fridge</span>
              <p class="mt-1 leading-relaxed text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400">
                Leveraging the <ConceptChip id="actor-model" label="Actor Model" icon="🧊" />, each fridge is represented by an in-memory entity that serializes reservation and inventory changes sequentially. In the team's original words to the judges: <em>"We propose using the actor pattern because we have natural actors in real life: the fridges. A user always purchases from a specific fridge, and a meal cannot magically hop from one fridge to another."</em> Executing at CPU speed in memory eliminates race conditions and avoids heavy relational database table locks.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="p-4 rounded-xl border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 shadow-xs transition-colors">
              <span class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 font-mono text-emerald-800 dark:text-emerald-400">2. Event Sourcing with Versioned Aggregates</span>
              <p class="mt-1 leading-relaxed text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400">
                Orders are stored as append-only immutable event streams (<a href="https://github.com/TheKataLog/ArchColider/blob/master/4.ADRs/007%20Event%20sourcing%20usage.md" class="text-emerald-700 dark:text-emerald-400 underline font-mono">ADR 007</a>) using optimistic concurrency checks. If a billing dispute occurs, the system reconstructs the exact sequence of events that produced the state.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="p-4 rounded-xl border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 shadow-xs transition-colors">
              <span class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 font-mono text-emerald-800 dark:text-emerald-400">3. Log-Based Message Streaming</span>
              <p class="mt-1 leading-relaxed text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400">
                Orders publish domain events to RabbitMQ with at-least-once delivery guarantees (<a href="https://github.com/TheKataLog/ArchColider/blob/master/4.ADRs/008%20At%20least%20once%20delivery%20for%20ready%20to%20pay%20order.md" class="text-emerald-700 dark:text-emerald-400 underline font-mono">ADR 008</a>). Downstream consumers process tasks at their own pace without competing for resources with checkout.
              </p>
            </div>

            <!-- Tactic 4: The Inhibition Window -->
            <div class="p-4 rounded-xl border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 shadow-xs transition-colors space-y-2">
              <span class="font-bold text-xs font-mono uppercase text-emerald-800 dark:text-emerald-400 flex items-center gap-1.5">
                <span>⏱️</span>
                <span>The Inhibition Window: Clean Cancellations Gmail-Style (InformationModels.md)</span>
              </span>
              <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 leading-relaxed">
                To prevent chaotic compensating transactions and non-refundable payment gateway fees upon impulsive cancellations, ArchColider implemented an <ConceptChip id="inhibit-window" label="Inhibition Window (Gmail Undo Send)" icon="⏱️" />: buffering customer orders for 10-30 seconds in the backend before charging Stripe. Immediate user cancellations abort locally at $0 cost without calling banking APIs.
              </p>
            </div>

            <!-- Tactic 5: Pragmatic CRDT Rejection -->
            <div class="p-4 rounded-xl border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 shadow-xs transition-colors space-y-2">
              <span class="font-bold text-xs font-mono uppercase text-emerald-800 dark:text-emerald-400 flex items-center gap-1.5">
                <span>📊</span>
                <span>Radical Pragmatism: Rejecting CRDTs for Google Sheets (Concurrency.md)</span>
              </span>
              <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 leading-relaxed">
                For the promotional campaign module, the team avoided distributed state sync algorithms (<ConceptChip id="crdt-pragmatism" label="CRDT Rejection vs Spreadsheets" icon="📊" />): <em>"From the operator perspective we don't want to complicate it involving CRDTs... preparing campaigns in Google Spreadsheets or Excel is perfectly sufficient."</em>
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

