        <!-- 9.5: Risks and Sensitive Points -->
        <div class="space-y-4">
          <h3 class="text-lg sm:text-xl font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 flex items-center gap-2">
            <span>9.5 Documented Risk Register and Sensitive Points</span>
          </h3>
          <p class="leading-relaxed text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 text-sm sm:text-base">
            In their <a href="https://github.com/TheKataLog/ArchColider/blob/master/2.SolutionBackground/RisksAndSensitivePoints.md" target="_blank" class="text-emerald-700 dark:text-emerald-400 underline font-mono font-semibold">RisksAndSensitivePoints.md</a> document, ArchColider cataloged failure scenarios and concrete mitigations:
          </p>

          <div class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-3 text-xs sm:text-sm">
            <div class="p-4 rounded-xl border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 shadow-xs transition-colors">
              <span class="font-bold text-red-600 dark:text-red-400 font-mono">Stale Catalog Display vs. Actual Stock</span>
              <p class="text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-1 leading-relaxed">
                A user views an in-stock meal on their mobile app that was already physically taken from the fridge. <span class="font-semibold text-emerald-700 dark:text-emerald-400">Mitigation:</span> Optimistic catalog browsing in the mobile client for fast UI feedback, paired with mandatory real-time stock verification against the in-memory fridge actor at the exact moment of payment authorization.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="p-4 rounded-xl border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 shadow-xs transition-colors">
              <span class="font-bold text-red-600 dark:text-red-400 font-mono">The Dev vs. Prod Parity Trap (1:1 Problem)</span>
              <p class="text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-1 leading-relaxed">
                Running a single instance in local development masks concurrency races and distributed deadlocks. <span class="font-semibold text-emerald-700 dark:text-emerald-400">Mitigation:</span> Running at least 2 instances of business services (<ConceptChip id="dev-prod-parity-concurrency" label="1:1 Concurrency Parity Trap" icon="👥" />) behind a local reverse proxy to surface shared-state memory issues and locking bugs before deployment.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="p-4 rounded-xl border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 shadow-xs transition-colors">
              <span class="font-bold text-red-600 dark:text-red-400 font-mono">Breaking Changes in Event Message Contracts</span>
              <p class="text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-1 leading-relaxed">
                Schema evolution in RabbitMQ breaking existing consumer services. <span class="font-semibold text-emerald-700 dark:text-emerald-400">Mitigation:</span> Backwards and forwards compatible serialization rules with sunset headers on deprecated fields.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="p-4 rounded-xl border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 shadow-xs transition-colors">
              <span class="font-bold text-red-600 dark:text-red-400 font-mono">Review Bombing Partner Ghost Kitchens</span>
              <p class="text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-1 leading-relaxed">
                Malicious review spam targeting third-party kitchens. <span class="font-semibold text-emerald-700 dark:text-emerald-400">Mitigation:</span> Customer review submissions are restricted to users with settled purchases recorded in the event store.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- 9.6: Interactive ADR Compendium -->
        <div class="space-y-4">
          <div class="border-t border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pt-6">
            <h3 class="text-lg sm:text-xl font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 flex items-center gap-2">
              <span>9.6 Complete Interactive Compendium of the 16 ADRs</span>
            </h3>
            <p class="leading-relaxed text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 text-sm sm:text-base mt-2">
              ArchColider adopted Michael Nygard's format (<a href="https://github.com/TheKataLog/ArchColider/blob/master/4.ADRs/001%20We%20are%20using%20ADR%20(template).md" class="text-emerald-700 dark:text-emerald-400 underline font-mono font-semibold">ADR 001</a>) to document every system decision. Explore the full record of all 16 decisions (Accepted, Proposed, Declined, Draft) below:
            </p>
          </div>

          <!-- Interactive ADRs Component (English) -->
          <ArchColiderAdrs lang="en" />

          <p class="leading-relaxed text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 text-sm sm:text-base mt-4">
            ArchColider's thoroughness demonstrates how to formalize system design without ambiguity. However, applying these insights to everyday engineering work does not require writing sixteen ADRs on Day 1: it requires building consistent architectural decision-making habits.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- Section 10 -->
      <section id="guia-campo" class="space-y-6 pt-2">
        <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-3">
          <span class="text-xs font-mono font-bold text-emerald-700 dark:text-emerald-400 uppercase tracking-wider">Section 10</span>
          <h2 class="text-2xl sm:text-3xl font-extrabold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 mt-1">Field Guide: Practical Principles for Real-World Projects</h2>
        </div>

        <p class="leading-relaxed text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 text-sm sm:text-base">
          Beyond the kata competition, analyzing these proposals yields four practical principles for production systems:
        </p>

        <div class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-4 text-xs sm:text-sm">
          <div class="p-4 rounded-xl border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 shadow-xs transition-colors">
            <div class="font-bold text-emerald-800 dark:text-emerald-400 font-mono mb-1">1. Concise ADRs for High-Reversal-Cost Choices</div>
            <p class="text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 leading-relaxed">Focus formal documentation on decisions with high costs of reversal: datastore engine selection, transaction boundaries (ACID vs. eventual consistency), or third-party vendor integrations.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="p-4 rounded-xl border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 shadow-xs transition-colors">
            <div class="font-bold text-emerald-800 dark:text-emerald-400 font-mono mb-1">2. Early Proof of Concept for External Dependencies</div>
            <p class="text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 leading-relaxed">Before finalizing architectural blueprints or committing project deadlines, validate real-world third-party API behavior and physical hardware limitations through targeted proof of concepts.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="p-4 rounded-xl border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 shadow-xs transition-colors">
            <div class="font-bold text-emerald-800 dark:text-emerald-400 font-mono mb-1">3. Logical Modularity Before Physical Distribution</div>
            <p class="text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 leading-relaxed">Starting with a modular monolith featuring enforced domain boundaries minimizes initial operational complexity while preserving the ability to extract microservices when true traffic scale justifies it.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="p-4 rounded-xl border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 shadow-xs transition-colors">
            <div class="font-bold text-emerald-800 dark:text-emerald-400 font-mono mb-1">4. Explicit Decision Tie-Breakers Up Front</div>
            <p class="text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 leading-relaxed">Establishing clear criteria (such as cognitive simplicity over premature optimization) provides an objective benchmark for resolving technical disagreements and evaluating implementation tradeoffs.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- Section 11 -->
      <section id="sintesis" class="space-y-6 pt-2">
        <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-3">
          <span class="text-xs font-mono font-bold text-emerald-700 dark:text-emerald-400 uppercase tracking-wider">Section 11</span>
          <h2 class="text-2xl sm:text-3xl font-extrabold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 mt-1">Final Synthesis & Original Repositories</h2>
        </div>

        <p class="leading-relaxed text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 text-sm sm:text-base">
          Ultimately, software architecture is the discipline of making trade-offs explicit: consciously choosing which quality attributes to prioritize at each stage of a system's life, and transparently documenting the consequences of every choice.
        </p>

        <div class="rounded-xl border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 p-6 space-y-4 shadow-xs transition-colors">
          <div class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 font-mono uppercase text-xs tracking-wider">Original GitHub Repositories (TheKataLog)</div>
